         "A material is soft if it can be easily compressed, bent, or scratched with a fingernail (e.g. sponge, cotton, chalk, wax); a material is hard if it resists compression, bending, and cannot be easily scratched (e.g. iron, stone, glass, diamond).",
         "Hardness is an empirical mechanical property tested by the scratch test: a harder material can scratch a softer material, but a softer material cannot scratch a harder one.",
         "1. Mechanical test 1: Compression. Try pressing with fingers. Soft materials deform easily; hard materials resist deformation.\n2. Mechanical test 2: Scratch test. Try scratching with a steel nail. Chalk and candle wax scratch easily (soft); iron and stone do not scratch (hard).\n3. Relative scale: lead is a soft metal; diamond is the hardest natural substance known.\n4. Conclude hardness measures resistance to surface indentation and scratching.",
