         "Miscible liquids mix completely and uniformly with water forming a single continuous layer (e.g. vinegar, lemon juice, milk); immiscible liquids do not mix with water and separate into two distinct layers upon standing (e.g. mustard oil, kerosene, petrol).",
         "Chemical polarity dictates liquid mixing: polar liquids (water, vinegar) dissolve in each other ('like dissolves like'), while non-polar oils are hydrophobic and float on top as a separate layer.",
         "1. Miscible test: add lemon juice to water and stir; it forms a single, uniform, homogeneous liquid phase.\n2. Immiscible test: add mustard oil to water and stir; tiny oil droplets disperse temporarily, but soon coalesce and float to the top as a distinct immiscible layer.\n3. Phase boundary: immiscible liquids display a clear visible meniscus separating the two liquids.\n4. Conclude miscibility reflects intermolecular chemical compatibility.",
