         "Flotation depends on density (mass per unit volume) relative to water, not total weight; wood is less dense than water so it floats, whereas iron is denser than water so even a tiny pin sinks.",
         "Density measures how tightly matter is packed inside a space. Water has a density of 1 g/cm³. Wood has a lower density (~0.6-0.8 g/cm³), so water's buoyant upward force supports it. Solid iron has a density of 7.8 g/cm³, plunging straight to the bottom.",
         "1. Identify common misconception: heavy objects sink, light objects float.\n2. Counter-example: 500 kg wooden log floats; 1 gram iron pin sinks.\n3. Define density: Density = Mass ÷ Volume.\n4. Water density standard: 1.0 g/cm³.\n5. Comparison rule: If object density < 1.0 g/cm³, it floats; if object density > 1.0 g/cm³, it sinks.\n6. Conclude relative density, not total mass, governs buoyancy and flotation.",
