         "Translucent materials allow light to pass through only partially, scattering the light so that objects on the other side can be seen but not clearly (e.g. frosted glass, tracing paper, butter paper); rubbing a drop of oil onto white paper makes it translucent.",
         "Light enters translucent materials, but internal microscopic irregularities, fibres, or crystals scatter the light rays in chaotic directions, blurring and obscuring the optical image.",
         "1. Optical behavior: partial light transmission with significant internal scattering (diffusion).\n2. Visual perception: light passes through, but distinct shapes, edges, and details cannot be identified.\n3. Everyday examples: bathroom frosted glass windows, butter paper, tracing paper, tissue paper, dense fog.\n4. Oil paper transformation: oil fills microscopic air pockets between paper cellulose fibres, matching their refractive index and allowing partial light transmission (translucency).",
