    # 31..50: Misconceptions and corrective teaching (20)
    misc_data = [
        ("Heavy objects always sink", "A student drops a 10 kg dry wooden log and a 0.5 gram sewing needle into a lake, asserting: 'The 10 kg log will sink to the bottom like an anchor and the 0.5 g needle will float on top.'",
         "Student believes total weight is the sole determinant of sinking and floating.",
         "The 10 kg wooden log floats while the tiny 0.5 g needle sinks; sinking and floating depend entirely on density (mass per unit volume) relative to water, not total weight.",
         "The wooden log has a density (~0.7 g/cm³) lower than water, displacing its weight easily. The steel needle has a density (~7.8 g/cm³) nearly eight times greater than water, sinking immediately.",
         "1. Confront the prediction: test with real water tank.\n2. Observe result: massive log floats with part above water; tiny needle plunges instantly to the bottom.\n3. Formulate rule: compare density of object with density of water (1.0 g/cm³).\n4. Wood density < 1.0 g/cm³ -> Floats regardless of whether it weighs 1 gram or 1,000 kilograms.\n5. Steel density > 1.0 g/cm³ -> Sinks regardless of whether it weighs 0.1 gram or 100 kilograms.",
         "Believing total gravitational mass governs flotation rather than relative volumetric density.",
         "An enormous ocean cruise ship weighs 100,000 tonnes and floats; a single tiny pebble weighs 2 grams and sinks.",
         "Physical activity", "Drop an iron sewing pin and an entire raw carrot into water: record which sinks and which floats.",
         "Decouple total mass from relative density using direct physical counter-examples."),

        ("All liquids mix together smoothly", "A student pours mustard oil into a glass of water, asserting: 'All liquids are wet fluids, so if I stir hard with a spoon, they will permanently dissolve into one liquid.'",
         "Student believes liquid state guarantees miscibility.",
         "No matter how vigorously you stir, mustard oil and water will never dissolve in each other; they are immiscible liquids that separate into two distinct layers within minutes, with the oil floating on top.",
         "Water molecules are highly polar and attract each other strongly with hydrogen bonds, squeezing out non-polar oil molecules. The oil droplets coalesce and float because oil is less dense than water.",
         "1. Stirring effect: mechanical agitation shears oil into tiny temporary droplets (a temporary emulsion).\n2. Intermolecular forces: water molecules attract each other strongly, excluding non-polar lipid molecules.\n3. Coalescence: as kinetic stirring ceases, oil droplets bump into each other and merge into larger pools.\n4. Phase separation: within 3 to 5 minutes, a crisp horizontal boundary (meniscus) forms separating water below and oil above.\n5. Conclude immiscibility is an unalterable chemical property governed by molecular polarity.",
         "Assuming mechanical stirring can override thermodynamic chemical immiscibility.",
         "Shaking marbles and ping-pong balls in a box mixes them temporarily; gravity soon separates ping-pong balls to the top.",
         "Physical activity", "Stir cooking oil in water vigorously with a fork; time how many seconds it takes for two clear layers to reappear.",
         "Teach molecular polarity and phase separation through direct timing experiments."),

        ("Air is empty nothingness", "A student holds up an empty glass tumbler and says: 'Look, this tumbler has zero matter inside it; it is completely empty.'",
         "Student thinks invisible air is pure empty space lacking physical matter.",
         "The tumbler is completely full of invisible air; air is real matter made of gas molecules (nitrogen, oxygen) that occupies space, has mass, and exerts physical pressure.",
         "You can prove air occupies space by pushing an inverted glass tumbler straight down into a bucket of water: water cannot enter because trapped air fills the volume, until you tilt the glass to let air bubbles escape.",
         "1. Physical definition of matter: anything that has mass and occupies space.\n2. Invisible nature of air: transparent gases transmit visible light, making gas molecules invisible to naked eye.\n3. Inverted tumbler experiment: push upside-down tumbler vertically into water bucket -> water cannot enter.\n4. Tilt tumbler: giant air bubbles gurgle out, and water rushes in to replace the escaping air volume.\n5. Conclude air is a real physical substance occupying space.",
         "Equating visual transparency and invisibility with absolute non-existence of matter.",
         "You cannot see clean glass in a window, but if you walk into it, you bump your nose; air is real matter all around us.",
         "Physical activity", "Push an inverted plastic cup into a bucket of water: observe whether the tissue paper inside stays dry.",
         "Demonstrate air volume displacement using the classic inverted tumbler experiment."),

        ("Clear materials are always glass", "A student points to a transparent water bottle and says: 'This bottle is made of glass because I can see right through it.'",
         "Student assumes transparency is an exclusive proprietary property of glass.",
         "The bottle is made of transparent plastic (PET / Polycarbonate); many completely different materials can be transparent, including clear plastics, cellophane, clean water, air, and diamond.",
         "Transparency is an optical property shared by diverse materials across different chemical families. Material identity must be verified using weight, flexibility, thermal feel, and shatter resistance.",
         "1. Optical property vs Chemical composition: transparency occurs in glass, plastics, liquids, gases, and mineral crystals.\n2. Tactile diagnostics: tap the bottle with a pencil (plastic produces a dull thud, glass produces a sharp musical chime).\n3. Thermal test: glass feels cold to initial touch due to higher thermal conductivity; plastic feels neutral/warm.\n4. Flexibility test: squeeze gently (plastic flexes; glass is completely rigid).\n5. Conclude transparency alone does not establish material identity.",
         "Using a single optical property (transparency) to jump to a rigid material conclusion.",
         "A white shirt, white milk, and white chalk are all white, but they are completely different materials.",
         "None", "How can you tell whether a clear transparent ruler is made of plastic or mineral glass without breaking it?",
         "Teach multi-sensory diagnostic testing (sound, weight, thermal feel) beyond visual appearance."),

        ("All shiny objects are metals", "A student looks at a shiny plastic toy car, a glossy ceramic plate, and a polished polished apple, asserting: 'All three are metals because they have bright shine.'",
         "Student confuses glossy surface reflection with true metallic lustre.",
         "They are non-metals with smooth glossy surfaces; true metallic lustre is an inherent property of pure metals visible throughout the material, especially across freshly cut or scratched surfaces, whereas plastic and apples only have surface glaze.",
         "Non-metallic shine is specular reflection from a smooth surface coat (wax on an apple, glaze on ceramic, smooth mold on plastic). If you cut an apple or a plastic toy, the interior does not have metallic lustre.",
         "1. Surface reflection (gloss): smooth non-metals reflect light like a mirror due to surface smoothness.\n2. Metallic lustre: intrinsic quantum-mechanical property of metals due to free conduction band electrons reflecting all light wavelengths.\n3. The Cut Test: cut the material with a blade. Pure metals (lead, aluminium) show bright metallic gleam across the cut face.\n4. Non-metal cut face: cutting a glossy plastic toy or red apple reveals dull, non-lustrous interior material.\n5. Conclude true lustre is an inherent bulk metallic property, not a superficial surface polish.",
         "Equating shiny external surface finish with intrinsic metallic classification.",
         "Putting shoe polish on a wooden shoe makes it shine, but the wood never turns into metal.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a freshly cut potato versus a freshly cut aluminium wire under a bright lamp.",
         "Establish the freshly-cut surface test as the definitive benchmark for metallic lustre."),

        ("Solubility has no limit", "A student adds ten cups of sugar to one small cup of water, insisting: 'Water can dissolve infinite amounts of sugar if you just keep stirring for hours.'",
         "Student believes water has infinite dissolving capacity.",
         "Water has a strict chemical limit to how much solute it can dissolve at a given temperature; once it reaches its saturation point, no more sugar can dissolve, and excess crystals remain at the bottom as a saturated solution.",
         "Liquid water has a fixed number of intermolecular spaces. Once every water molecule is bonded to dissolved solute molecules, the solution becomes saturated, and any additional solute simply precipitates out.",
         "1. Molecular mechanics: water dissolves sugar by surrounding solute molecules in hydration shells.\n2. Finite volume: 100 mL of room-temperature water can dissolve approx. 200 grams of sugar.\n3. Saturation threshold: once 200 g is reached, the solution is chemically saturated.\n4. Precipitation: the 201st gram cannot find free water molecules and settles permanently at the bottom regardless of stirring.\n5. Temperature modulation: heating the water expands intermolecular spaces, allowing it to dissolve additional solute.",
         "Believing a solvent has an infinite capacity to dissolve matter.",
         "A bus has 40 seats; once all 40 seats are occupied, no more passengers can sit down, no matter how long the bus waits.",
         "Physical activity", "Add salt spoon-by-spoon to half a glass of water, stirring each time, until crystals no longer dissolve; record the spoon count.",
         "Introduce the concept of a saturated solution through hands-on spoon counting."),

        ("Paper is waterproof if thick", "A student wraps a wet ice cube in ten layers of thick brown cardboard, saying: 'Because cardboard is thick and heavy, water will never soak through.'",
         "Student confuses material thickness with water impermeability.",
         "Thickness only delays water penetration; cardboard is made of hydrophilic plant cellulose fibres that absorb water by capillary action, meaning the melting ice will eventually soak through and turn the cardboard soggy and weak.",
         "Unless a material is chemically hydrophobic or coated with an impermeable plastic/wax barrier, adding more layers of porous cellulose simply absorbs more water without becoming waterproof.",
         "1. Chemical nature of cardboard: compressed wood pulp cellulose.\n2. Water affinity: cellulose has polar hydroxyl groups that attract water molecules.\n3. Capillary wicking: porous spaces draw liquid water through capillary action.\n4. Time-delay effect: ten layers take longer to soak through than one layer, but permeability remains 100% porous.\n5. Waterlogged result: hydrogen bonds between paper fibres break, causing cardboard to collapse into mush.",
         "Confusing transient time delay with absolute water impermeability.",
         "Stacking ten dry sponges together stops water for a few minutes, but eventually all ten sponges become soaking wet.",
         "None", "What thin coating is applied to paper milk cartons (Tetra Paks) to make them genuinely waterproof?",
         "Teach the difference between porous bulk absorption and impermeable barrier coatings."),

        ("Mirrors are transparent glass", "A student claims: 'A bathroom mirror is transparent because it is made of glass.'",
         "Student confuses the clear front glass pane with the opaque reflective mirror backing.",
         "A mirror is an opaque object; while the front layer is protective transparent glass, the back surface is coated with an opaque, highly reflective layer of silver or aluminium metal and protective dark paint that completely blocks light.",
         "If a mirror were transparent, light would pass right through it into the wall behind, and you would see through the wall instead of seeing your own reflection.",
         "1. Anatomy of a mirror: front sheet of transparent glass + back layer of reflective silver/aluminium + protective dark enamel paint.\n2. Light pathway: light enters front glass, strikes the opaque silver metal backing, and reflects 100% back to your eyes.\n3. Light transmission: hold a bright flashlight against the face of a mirror in a dark room; zero light passes through the back wall.\n4. Conclude a mirror functions as an opaque reflector, not a transparent window.",
         "Failing to recognize the opaque metal backing that makes a mirror reflect light.",
         "A glass picture frame with a solid black metal sheet inside: you cannot see through the frame.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a cross-section of a mirror showing the front glass, reflective silver backing, and paint seal.",
         "Dissect mirror anatomy to prove optical opacity."),

        ("Ice is heavier than water because it is solid", "A student argues: 'Solids are always denser and heavier than liquids, so ice cubes must sink to the bottom of a drinking glass.'",
         "Student assumes solids must always be denser than their corresponding liquids.",
         "Ice is a rare, remarkable exception: liquid water expands as it freezes into ice, creating an open crystalline lattice with empty spaces that makes solid ice less dense (~0.92 g/cm³) than liquid water (~1.0 g/cm³), causing ice to float.",
         "Almost all substances contract when solidifying. Water exhibits anomalous expansion below 4°C, locking hydrogen-bonded molecules into an open hexagonal cage that takes up more volume, lowering its density.",
         "1. General physics rule: in most matter (iron, wax), solid molecules pack tighter than liquid molecules -> solid sinks in liquid.\n2. Anomalous expansion of water: as water cools from 4°C to 0°C, hydrogen bonds force molecules into an open hexagonal crystal lattice.\n3. Density comparison: Liquid water at 4°C = 1.00 g/cm³; Solid ice at 0°C = 0.92 g/cm³.\n4. Flotation outcome: ice floats with approx. 9% of its volume protruding above the water surface.\n5. Ecological significance: freezing ponds freeze from top to bottom, allowing fish to survive in liquid water beneath the ice shield.",
         "Assuming the solid state is universally denser than the liquid state for all substances in nature.",
         "A metal cage has lots of empty space inside; an ice crystal is an open molecular cage filled with empty space.",
         "Physical activity", "Drop an ice cube into a glass of water: observe which part floats above the water line.",
         "Teach water's anomalous density and its profound planetary ecological importance."),

        ("Dissolved substances are gone forever", "A student dissolves 5 spoons of salt in a glass of water and says: 'The salt was destroyed and turned into pure water; it is impossible to ever get the salt back.'",
         "Student confuses physical dissolution with chemical destruction of matter.",
         "The salt is still 100% physically present in the water, broken into invisible individual ions; if you heat and boil away the water (evaporation), pure white salt crystals will remain behind in the vessel.",
         "Dissolution is a physical change, not a chemical destruction. The mass of the salt water equals the mass of the water plus the mass of the salt, and the original salt can be recovered completely by evaporation.",
         "1. Conservation of mass: 100 g water + 10 g salt = 110 g saltwater solution.\n2. Invisible state: salt crystals dissociate into microscopic sodium and chloride ions that fit between water molecules.\n3. Taste proof: the water tastes salty, proving the substance is actively present.\n4. Separation method: heat the beaker to 100°C; water evaporates as steam; non-volatile salt crystals recrystallize in the pan.\n5. Conclude dissolved matter is conserved and completely recoverable.",
         "Believing that invisible dissolved substances have ceased to exist.",
         "Dissolving salt is like putting small coins into your pocket: you cannot see the coins from outside, but they are still in your pocket.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What white substance remains in the porcelain evaporating dish after saltwater is boiled to dryness?",
         "Connect solubility to the Law of Conservation of Mass and physical separation."),

        ("Rubber is a liquid because it stretches", "A student stretches a rubber band and rolls it into a ball, arguing: 'Because rubber changes shape easily when pulled, it must be a liquid or semi-liquid.'",
         "Student confuses mechanical elasticity with the liquid state of matter.",
         "Rubber is a solid; it has a definite shape and volume at rest, does not flow like a liquid, and requires an external mechanical force to stretch its coiled polymer chains, snapping back when released (elasticity).",
         "Liquids take the shape of their container and flow under gravity. Elastic solids have cross-linked molecular chains that stretch under tension and spring back to their fixed resting shape.",
         "1. Definition of liquid: no fixed shape, fixed volume, flows under gravity, takes container shape.\n2. Elastic solid behavior: rubber band maintains its fixed looped shape on a table; does not flow or pool like water.\n3. Elasticity mechanism: natural polyisoprene polymer chains are tangled coils; pulling stretches the coils; releasing allows them to snap back.\n4. Conclude rubber is an elastic solid, not a liquid.",
         "Confusing flexible elasticity with fluid liquid flow.",
         "A steel spring stretches and bounces back, but a steel spring is 100% solid metal.",
         "None", "Does a rubber eraser take the shape of a pencil box like water takes the shape of a bottle?",
         "Enforce strict scientific criteria for the solid state (fixed volume, fixed resting shape)."),

        ("Cotton floats because it is lighter than water", "A student drops a fluffy dry cotton ball in water and it floats, concluding: 'Cotton is less dense than water and will float forever.'",
         "Student ignores the trapped air inside porous cotton.",
         "Pure cotton cellulose is actually denser than water (~1.5 g/cm³) and will sink once its fibres become completely waterlogged; dry cotton floats only because it traps a large volume of light air between its fluffy hairs.",
         "The cotton ball is a composite of cellulose fibres and trapped air. As water penetrates and expels the air, the true density of cellulose takes over, causing the soggy cotton ball to sink toward the bottom.",
         "1. Pure cellulose density: approx. 1.52 g/cm³ (denser than water's 1.0 g/cm³).\n2. Dry cotton ball structure: 90% air by volume trapped in fluffy labyrinth of fibres.\n3. Initial state: composite density < 1.0 g/cm³ -> Floats high on water.\n4. Capillary soaking: water displaces air pockets; fibres absorb water and collapse.\n5. Final state: air-free waterlogged cotton ball has density > 1.0 g/cm³ -> Sinks.",
         "Assuming initial temporary flotation reflects the true intrinsic density of the solid material.",
         "A dry paper boat floats while full of air, but sinks to the bottom as soon as water fills it.",
         "Physical activity", "Watch a floating dry cotton ball: poke it underwater with a pencil until all bubbles escape and observe where it goes.",
         "Differentiate between temporary air-entrapment buoyancy and true intrinsic material density."),

        ("All plastics are soft and melt easily", "A student claims: 'All plastics are soft like shopping bags and melt into gooey wax in hot water.'",
         "Student generalizes thin polyethylene films to all plastic polymers.",
         "Plastics exhibit immense diversity: thin polythene bags are soft, but engineering plastics like Bakelite (switchboards), Kevlar (bulletproof vests), and Teflon (non-stick pans) are rock-hard, withstand extreme heat, and never melt in boiling water.",
         "Polymer chemistry designs plastics for specific applications: thermoplastics have weak intermolecular bonds that melt with heat; thermosets have rigid 3D cross-linked networks that resist heat and high mechanical stress.",
         "1. Range of hardness: soft polyethylene cling wrap versus rock-hard polycarbonate bulletproof shields.\n2. Range of heat resistance: polystyrene melts at 100°C; Teflon withstands 260°C; Bakelite does not melt at all.\n3. Molecular architecture: linear chains vs densely cross-linked polymer matrices.\n4. Conclude 'plastic' is a vast family of thousands of diverse synthetic polymers with radically different properties.",
         "Treating 'plastic' as a single uniform substance with identical properties.",
         "A tiny copper coin and a massive steel bridge are both metals; a soft cling film and a bulletproof shield are both plastics.",
         "None", "Which hard heat-resistant plastic is used to make electrical switch plates in your home?",
         "Broaden student understanding of polymer material diversity."),

        ("Chalk dissolves in water because it turns milky", "A student mixes chalk powder in water, sees the white milky liquid, and declares: 'The chalk dissolved because it turned white like milk.'",
         "Student confuses an insoluble suspended mixture with true molecular dissolution.",
         "Chalk powder does not dissolve in water; it forms an insoluble suspension of fine solid particles that scatter light (making it look milky), and if left undisturbed, all chalk particles will settle to the bottom as sediment.",
         "True solutions are transparent and never settle upon standing (e.g. sugar in water). Suspensions are cloudy, scatter light, and separate under gravity over time.",
         "1. True solution test: solute particles are at molecular scale (<1 nm), completely transparent, and never settle out.\n2. Suspension test: chalk particles are large (>1000 nm), reflecting and scattering light in all directions (cloudy/milky).\n3. Gravity test: let the milky chalk water sit undisturbed for 15 minutes.\n4. Result: clear water appears on top while a layer of white chalk powder settles at the bottom (sedimentation).\n5. Conclude chalk is completely insoluble in water.",
         "Equating cloudy white appearance with true chemical dissolution.",
         "Stirring fine mud in water makes muddy water, but the mud is not dissolved and settles to the bottom.",
         "Physical activity", "Mix chalk powder in water, let the glass sit for 15 minutes, and observe the bottom of the glass.",
         "Teach sedimentation and stability as diagnostic tests distinguishing solutions from suspensions."),

        ("Glass is a metal because it is hard and shiny", "A student observes a polished glass marble, noting that it is hard, shiny, and heavy, concluding: 'Glass is a newly discovered metal.'",
         "Student lists superficial traits (hard, shiny) without testing fundamental metallic properties.",
         "Glass is an inorganic non-metal (made of fused silica sand); unlike metals, glass is brittle (shatters on impact), cannot be hammered into sheets (non-malleable), cannot be drawn into wires (non-ductile), and is an electrical insulator.",
         "Metals are defined by crystalline electron bonding that imparts malleability, ductility, and electrical conductivity. Glass is an amorphous brittle insulator that shatters under tensile shock.",
         "1. Electrical test: metals conduct electricity; glass blocks current completely (insulator).\n2. Mechanical impact test: hitting an iron nail with a hammer flattens it (malleable); hitting a glass marble shatters it into shards (brittle).\n3. Thermal conduction: metals conduct heat rapidly; glass is a poor conductor of heat.\n4. Raw material: glass is made by melting silicon dioxide (quartz sand) with soda ash and limestone.",
         "Misclassifying non-metals as metals based on superficial hardness and surface shine.",
         "A hard rock is shiny and heavy, but a rock is definitely not a metal.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What happens when you strike an iron nail with a hammer versus when you strike a glass marble?",
         "Enforce the fundamental defining criteria of metals: malleability, ductility, and conductivity."),

        ("Diamond is unbreakable by a hammer", "A student claims: 'Diamond is the hardest substance on Earth, so if you smash a diamond with a heavy steel hammer on an anvil, the hammer will break and the diamond will be undamaged.'",
         "Student confuses scientific 'hardness' (scratch resistance) with 'toughness' (fracture resistance).",
         "A steel hammer will easily shatter a diamond into tiny pieces; diamond is the hardest material (resists scratching), but it is brittle and possesses cleavage planes along which it fractures under blunt mechanical impact.",
         "In materials science, hardness is resistance to surface scratching (diamond is 10 on the Mohs scale). Toughness is resistance to blunt impact shock. Diamond has low impact toughness along its octahedral cleavage planes.",
         "1. Mohs hardness: measures scratch resistance. Diamond scratches every other material on Earth.\n2. Cleavage planes: diamond crystal lattice has four directional planes of weakness where carbon bonds align.\n3. Impact shock: striking with a steel hammer concentrates tensile shear shock along cleavage planes.\n4. Fracture result: the diamond shatters into multiple fragments.\n5. Conclude hardness does not mean shatterproof impact resistance.",
         "Equating scratch hardness with shatterproof mechanical toughness.",
         "A ceramic plate is hard and resists being scratched with a fork, but if you drop it on the floor, it shatters.",
         "None", "Which mineral property measures resistance to scratching: hardness or toughness?",
         "Teach precise scientific distinction between scratch hardness and impact toughness."),

        ("Wood always floats no matter what species", "A student claims: 'Every piece of wood from every tree on Earth will always float on water.'",
         "Student assumes all botanical timber has identical density below 1.0 g/cm³.",
         "While most common woods float, dense hardwoods like African Blackwood, Lignum Vitae, and Indian Ebony have densities exceeding 1.2 to 1.3 g/cm³, causing them to sink straight to the bottom of water like stones.",
         "The density of wood depends on how tightly cellulose fibres are packed and the thickness of plant cell walls relative to air vessel cavities. Ironwoods have minimal internal air space, making them denser than water.",
         "1. Common timber density: Pine (~0.5 g/cm³), Teak (~0.65 g/cm³), Oak (~0.75 g/cm³) -> all float.\n2. Extreme density timber: Lignum Vitae (1.26 g/cm³), Ebony (1.15 g/cm³), Snakewood (1.30 g/cm³).\n3. Flotation rule: density > 1.0 g/cm³ -> Sinks in water.\n4. Conclude timber density varies across plant species from 0.15 g/cm³ (balsa) to 1.3 g/cm³ (ironwood).",
         "Overgeneralizing biological materials without recognizing natural species variation.",
         "Balsa wood is light like Styrofoam; ebony wood is dense like a bowling ball.",
         "None", "Which ultra-lightweight wood has a density of only 0.15 g/cm³ and is used to build toy model gliders?",
         "Introduce balsa wood and density variation across biological species."),

        ("Liquids have zero weight in water", "A student holding a plastic bucket underwater in a swimming pool says: 'The water in the bucket has zero weight because I cannot feel any heaviness until I pull it out of the pool.'",
         "Student confuses apparent buoyant weight loss with zero physical mass.",
         "Water inside the submerged bucket has real gravitational mass and weight, but because the surrounding water exerts an equal upward buoyant force (Archimedes' principle), the apparent weight you feel with your hands drops to zero.",
         "Buoyancy counteracts gravity. When a bucket of water is surrounded by water, the upward pressure of the displaced liquid balances the downward gravitational pull of the water inside the bucket, creating apparent weightlessness.",
         "1. True physical weight: gravity pulls downward on the water mass (W = m × g).\n2. Buoyant force: surrounding water exerts upward buoyant pressure equal to the weight of displaced water.\n3. Net apparent force: Upward Buoyancy = Downward Weight -> Net force felt by hands is near zero.\n4. Lifting out of water: once the bucket breaks the water surface, buoyant support vanishes, and you feel the full 10 kg weight.",
         "Equating the neutral buoyancy of a submerged fluid with disappearance of physical mass.",
         "Floating in a swimming pool makes your body feel weightless, but your body weight has not vanished.",
         "Physical activity", "Lift a submerged mug of water in a bucket: notice the sudden heavy tug the instant it leaves the water.",
         "Teach Archimedes' principle and apparent weight in fluids."),

        ("Translucent materials block all shadows", "A student claims: 'Only completely opaque objects can cast shadows; translucent materials like frosted glass can never make a shadow.'",
         "Student thinks shadows require 100% total blockage of all light.",
         "Translucent materials do cast shadows; because they scatter and absorb part of the light rays, the region behind them receives less light than the surrounding area, forming a visible faint or soft-edged shadow.",
         "A shadow is simply any region where light is blocked or diminished relative to the brightly illuminated surroundings. It does not have to be pitch black; soft shadows are cast by tracing paper, fog, and frosted glass.",
         "1. Definition of shadow: a darker region formed when an object obstructs the path of light rays.\n2. Opaque shadow: blocks 100% of light -> produces a dark, sharp umbra shadow.\n3. Translucent shadow: blocks/scatters 40-70% of light -> produces a lighter, softer, diffused shadow.\n4. Proof: hold a piece of tracing paper between a torch and a white wall; a clearly visible rectangular grey shadow appears.",
         "Assuming shadows must be absolute pitch-black silhouettes.",
         "A thick storm cloud is translucent and lets some grey light through, but it casts a huge shadow over the whole city.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a torch shining on an opaque cardboard sheet versus a translucent tracing paper sheet onto a wall.",
         "Demonstrate gradient shadows and optical transmission variations."),

        ("Pure distilled water conducts electricity vigorously", "A student claims: 'If you drop an electric wire in a tub of 100% pure lab-distilled water, it will conduct electricity like a copper wire.'",
         "Student confuses pure chemical H2O with everyday mineral tap water.",
         "Completely pure distilled water is an electrical insulator that does not conduct electricity; everyday tap water conducts electricity only because it contains dissolved mineral salts and ions.",
         "Water molecules (H2O) are covalently bonded and do not have free ions or electrons to carry current. Conduction in water requires free charged mobile ions (Na+, Cl-, Ca2+) supplied by dissolved minerals or added salt.",
         "1. Pure distilled water (deionized): contains almost zero free dissolved ions; electrical conductivity is negligible (~0.05 μS/cm) -> behaves as an insulator.\n2. Domestic tap water / river water: contains dissolved salts (calcium bicarbonate, sodium chloride) -> mobile ions conduct current.\n3. Lab proof: a light bulb in a circuit with two electrodes in pure distilled water stays off; add half a spoon of common salt, and the bulb instantly glows brightly.\n4. Safety lesson: human sweat and bath water contain salts, making wet human skin highly conductive and vulnerable to electric shock.",
         "Believing water molecules themselves are electrical conductors.",
         "An empty highway has no cars moving; pure water has no free ions to carry electricity until you add mineral salt cars.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What happens to a circuit conductivity tester when table salt is stirred into a beaker of distilled water?",
         "Clarify the role of dissolved mineral ions in aqueous electrical conduction.")
    ]

