    # 51..65: Step-by-step reasoning / problem solving (15)
    step_data = [
        ("Step-by-step solubility testing of common substances", "Design a systematic laboratory protocol to test whether five unknown household samples (Sugar, Sand, Mustard oil, Vinegar, Chalk powder) are soluble, insoluble, miscible, or immiscible in water.",
         "Student dumps all five samples together into a single bucket of water.",
         "1. Take five separate clean beakers filled with 100 mL water. 2. Add 1 teaspoon of one sample to each beaker. 3. Stir vigorously for 1 minute. 4. Allow beakers to stand undisturbed for 5 minutes. 5. Observe transparency, sedimentation, or layer separation.",
         "Scientific testing requires isolated experimental trials, standardized solvent volumes, identical stirring times, and observation after a settling period.",
         "Step 1: Set up 5 clean beakers with 100 mL room-temperature water; label them A to E.\nStep 2: Add 1 teaspoon of Sugar to A, Sand to B, Mustard oil to C, Vinegar to D, Chalk powder to E.\nStep 3: Stir each beaker with a clean glass rod for exactly 60 seconds.\nStep 4: Let all beakers stand undisturbed on the bench for 5 minutes.\nStep 5: Record observations: A = clear, dissolved (Soluble solid); B = heavy grains settled at bottom (Insoluble solid); C = floating yellow layer (Immiscible liquid); D = uniform single clear phase (Miscible liquid); E = cloudy liquid with bottom sediment (Insoluble suspension).\nStep 6: Tabulate results in a clean scientific matrix.",
         "Failing to use separate beakers or failing to allow a settling period before observing.",
         "Testing five keys on five different doors one by one to see which key opens which door.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which sample in that test was an immiscible liquid that formed a floating layer?",
         "Drill standardized comparative laboratory protocols in materials testing."),

        ("Measuring density of irregular solid by water displacement", "Describe the step-by-step procedure to determine the volume, mass, and density of an irregular stone using a graduated measuring cylinder, water, and a digital balance.",
         "Student measures the stone with a wooden ruler.",
         "1. Weigh dry stone on digital balance (Mass = M). 2. Fill measuring cylinder with water to initial mark (V1). 3. Gently lower stone tied to thread into cylinder until submerged. 4. Record new water level (V2). 5. Stone volume = V2 - V1. 6. Density = Mass ÷ Volume.",
         "Archimedes' water displacement method measures the precise 3D volume of irregular objects that cannot be calculated with geometry formulas, allowing accurate density calculation.",
         "Step 1: Place clean, dry stone on an electronic balance; record mass M = 75.0 grams.\nStep 2: Pour water into a 100 mL graduated cylinder; read the lower meniscus at eye level: V1 = 50.0 mL.\nStep 3: Tie a thin cotton thread around the stone.\nStep 4: Tilt the cylinder slightly and slide the stone in gently without splashing water.\nStep 5: Record new elevated water meniscus level: V2 = 75.0 mL.\nStep 6: Calculate displaced volume: V = V2 - V1 = 75.0 - 50.0 = 25.0 mL (25.0 cm³).\nStep 7: Calculate density: D = Mass ÷ Volume = 75.0 g ÷ 25.0 cm³ = 3.0 g/cm³.\nStep 8: Conclude stone has a density of 3.0 g/cm³, explaining why it sinks in water (1.0 g/cm³).",
         "Splashing water during immersion or reading the top of the water meniscus instead of the bottom.",
         "Getting into a full bathtub makes the water overflow; the volume of overflow equals the volume of your body.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Why must you read the water level at the bottom of the curved meniscus rather than the top edges?",
         "Teach the water displacement method and quantitative density calculation."),

        ("Dichotomous key for sorting classroom materials", "Construct a step-by-step dichotomous identification key to classify four objects: Iron nail, Wooden block, Clear glass marble, and Candle wax.",
         "Student writes a confusing paragraph with no questions.",
         "Step 1: Does it sink in water? If Yes -> go to Step 2. If No -> go to Step 3. Step 2: Does it have metallic lustre? If Yes -> Iron nail; If No -> Glass marble. Step 3: Can it be easily scratched with a fingernail? If Yes -> Candle wax; If No -> Wooden block.",
         "A dichotomous key uses sequential pairs of contrasting questions based on observable physical properties to systematically identify and categorize materials.",
         "Step 1: Test buoyancy in water:\n  - Sinks in water (Density > 1.0 g/cm³) -> Group A (Iron nail, Glass marble)\n  - Floats on water (Density < 1.0 g/cm³) -> Group B (Wooden block, Candle wax)\nStep 2: Differentiate Group A using Lustre/Conductivity:\n  - Displays metallic lustre and conducts electricity -> Iron nail\n  - Transparent/translucent, non-conductive, brittle -> Glass marble\nStep 3: Differentiate Group B using Hardness (Scratch test):\n  - Soft, scratches easily with fingernail, melts at low heat -> Candle wax\n  - Hard, fibrous, resists fingernail scratch -> Wooden block\nStep 4: Verify that all four materials are uniquely identified.",
         "Using subjective or non-binary criteria that do not cleanly split samples into two groups.",
         "A choose-your-own-adventure book where each answer sends you down a specific path.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a branching flowchart diagram representing this 3-step dichotomous identification key.",
         "Teach systematic binary classification and diagnostic branching keys."),

        ("Step-by-step testing of optical transmission", "Outline the exact step-by-step method to classify three sheets (Clear plastic sheet, Butter paper, Cardboard) as transparent, translucent, or opaque using a lit torch bulb.",
         "Student looks at the sheets in a dark closet without any light source.",
         "1. Place a lit torch on a table facing a white wall in a darkened room. 2. Insert Sheet 1 between torch and wall. 3. Observe light reaching wall and whether the glowing torch bulb is visible through the sheet. 4. Repeat for Sheets 2 and 3.",
         "Optical transmission is evaluated by testing both light throughput (how much illumination passes through to a screen) and image clarity (whether the source can be distinctly recognized through the material).",
         "Step 1: Mount a torch light firmly on a stand facing a white cardboard screen 1 metre away in a dim room.\nStep 2: Test Sample 1 (Clear plastic sheet): bright circle of light hits the wall; looking through the sheet, the glowing torch filament is seen sharply -> Transparent.\nStep 3: Test Sample 2 (Butter paper): diffused, dim glow reaches the wall; looking through, only a blurred patch of light is seen with zero filament details -> Translucent.\nStep 4: Test Sample 3 (Cardboard): zero light reaches the wall; dark shadow cast; completely impossible to see torch -> Opaque.\nStep 5: Record optical category for each sample in a test report.",
         "Failing to observe both screen illumination and source image clarity.",
         "Testing sunglasses: looking through clear glasses, tinted sunglasses, and blindfolds.",
         "Physical activity", "Hold your hand between a flashlight and the wall: is your hand transparent, translucent, or opaque?",
         "Enforce systematic optical observation protocols using light source and screen."),

        ("Calculating float-sink threshold for composite objects", "A sealed plastic bottle has an internal volume of 500 cm³ and an empty mass of 50 grams. How many grams of water must be added inside the bottle so that it just begins to sink in a bucket of freshwater?",
         "Student guesses 500 grams without subtracting the bottle's mass.",
         "450 grams of water must be added. (For sinking, total density must exceed 1.0 g/cm³. For 500 cm³ volume, total mass must exceed 500 grams. Required water = 500 g - 50 g bottle = 450 g).",
         "An object sinks when its total average density exceeds the density of the surrounding liquid (1.0 g/cm³). Calculating the sinking threshold balances total composite mass against displaced volume.",
         "Step 1: Identify total external volume of sealed bottle: V = 500 cm³.\nStep 2: Density of water standard: D_water = 1.0 g/cm³.\nStep 3: Calculate maximum buoyant mass supported before sinking: M_max = V × D_water = 500 cm³ × 1.0 g/cm³ = 500 grams.\nStep 4: Identify mass of the empty bottle: M_bottle = 50 grams.\nStep 5: Calculate mass of water needed inside: M_water = M_max - M_bottle = 500 g - 50 g = 450 grams (450 mL).\nStep 6: Conclude adding more than 450 mL of water makes the bottle denser than water, causing it to sink.",
         "Forgetting to include the mass of the container itself in total composite density.",
         "A boat can carry a total weight of 500 kg before sinking; if the boat itself weighs 50 kg, it can hold 450 kg of cargo.",
         "None", "What happens to the bottle if exactly 200 grams of water is added inside it?",
         "Teach quantitative buoyancy thresholds and ballast calculations."),

        ("Investigating metal corrosion and lustre recovery", "An old iron nail is dull, rough, and coated with reddish-brown rust. Describe a 4-step laboratory procedure to restore its bright metallic lustre.",
         "Student washes the nail with plain cold water and expects rust to vanish.",
         "1. Inspect dull oxidized surface. 2. Rub surface vigorously with coarse sandpaper (emery paper) to scrape off the rust layer. 3. Wipe off loose dust with a clean dry cloth. 4. Observe the exposed inner iron surface: shiny metallic lustre is restored.",
         "Rust is a surface coating of hydrated iron oxide formed by atmospheric weathering. Mechanically abrading the brittle oxide layer exposes the unreacted bulk metal underneath, revealing its natural metallic lustre.",
         "Step 1: Visual observation: note that the outer rust layer is reddish-brown, powdery, non-lustrous, and brittle.\nStep 2: Mechanical abrasion: wrap a piece of medium-grit sandpaper (emery paper) tightly around the nail and rub vigorously back and forth.\nStep 3: Oxide removal: the brittle rust flakes off as brown powder, revealing the smooth silver-grey metal below.\nStep 4: Optical inspection: hold the sanded nail under a light lamp: clean metallic specular lustre is brightly visible.\nStep 5: Preservation step: apply a drop of machine oil to protect the freshly exposed lustre from re-oxidizing.",
         "Believing rust penetrates through the entire metal instantly, or that water alone dissolves rust.",
         "Scraping burnt crust off a slice of toast reveals the soft golden bread underneath.",
         "Physical activity", "Rub an old rusted iron nail with sandpaper: observe how quickly the shiny metallic grey color appears.",
         "Demonstrate that lustre is an intrinsic bulk metallic property beneath weathered surface oxides."),

        ("Separating a complex 4-component mixture by properties", "You have a dry mixture of Iron filings, Sand, Salt, and Sawdust. Design a sequential step-by-step separation plan using the distinct properties of each material.",
         "Student tries to pick each grain of sand with tweezers.",
         "1. Use a magnet to remove magnetic Iron filings. 2. Add water and stir: Sawdust floats (low density), Salt dissolves (soluble), Sand sinks (insoluble dense solid). 3. Skim floating sawdust off top. 4. Filter to collect sand. 5. Evaporate water to recover salt.",
         "Separation science exploits differences in physical properties: magnetism, relative density, solubility in water, and boiling points.",
         "Step 1: Magnetic separation: move a strong bar magnet wrapped in plastic wrap through the dry mixture; all Iron filings cling to the magnet -> separated.\nStep 2: Dissolution and Density separation: add remaining mixture (sand, salt, sawdust) to 200 mL of water and stir thoroughly.\nStep 3: Skimming: light sawdust has density < 1.0 g/cm³ and floats on the surface; skim it off with a fine mesh spoon and dry it -> separated.\nStep 4: Filtration: pour the remaining liquid through a funnel lined with filter paper; heavy insoluble sand grains are retained on filter paper -> separated.\nStep 5: Evaporation: pour the clear salt solution into a dish and boil until all water evaporates; dry salt crystals remain -> separated.\nStep 6: Verify all four components are recovered pure.",
         "Attempting separation steps in the wrong order (e.g. adding water before removing iron filings).",
         "A multi-stage sorting conveyor belt at a recycling plant that uses magnets, water tanks, and air blowers.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Why should you use the magnet first while the mixture is completely dry rather than after adding water?",
         "Teach multi-stage separation logic based on material property differentials."),

        ("Evaluating material suitability for cooking pots", "A designer suggests making cooking pots from: 1. Wood, 2. Paper, 3. Lead, 4. Stainless steel. Evaluate each material step by step and justify the final choice.",
         "Student picks paper because paper is light and easy to wash.",
         "Stainless steel is the only suitable choice. Wood catches fire and is an insulator; Paper burns instantly and leaks; Lead melts at low temperature (327°C) and is dangerously toxic; Stainless steel conducts heat, withstands high flames, does not melt, and is food-safe.",
         "Material selection requires satisfying multiple simultaneous constraints: thermal conductivity, melting point, structural rigidity, chemical non-toxicity, and flammability.",
         "Step 1: Evaluate Wood: catches fire at cooking temperatures (>250°C), poor thermal conductor -> completely rejected.\nStep 2: Evaluate Paper: flammable, structural integrity collapses when wet, burns at 233°C -> completely rejected.\nStep 3: Evaluate Lead: melts at 327°C on stove burners; highly toxic heavy metal that leaches into food causing brain poisoning -> completely rejected.\nStep 4: Evaluate Stainless Steel: high melting point (>1400°C), good thermal conductivity, mechanically rugged, zero toxic leaching -> chosen.\nStep 5: Conclude stainless steel is the optimal engineering material.",
         "Selecting materials based on a single convenience factor while ignoring safety, thermal limits, and toxicity.",
         "You cannot build a frying pan out of chocolate, no matter how delicious chocolate is.",
         "None", "Which toxic heavy metal was historically used for water pipes before scientists discovered its poisoning hazard?",
         "Teach rigorous multi-criteria engineering material selection and toxicology awareness."),

        ("Investigating gas solubility variation with temperature", "Describe the step-by-step observation when a beaker of cold tap water is heated on a tripod burner, explaining the origin of tiny bubbles before boiling begins.",
         "Student thinks the tiny bubbles appearing at 40°C are boiling steam bubbles.",
         "1. Pour cold tap water into a transparent glass beaker. 2. Place over a small flame. 3. Observe inner glass walls around 40-60°C: tiny bubbles appear and cling to glass. 4. These bubbles are dissolved air (oxygen/nitrogen) escaping because gases become less soluble as water warms.",
         "Unlike solids (whose solubility increases with heat), gases become less soluble in liquids as temperature rises. Heating increases the kinetic energy of dissolved gas molecules, causing them to break free from liquid water.",
         "Step 1: Set up a clean Pyrex beaker with 200 mL of cold tap water on a wire gauze and tripod stand.\nStep 2: Light Bunsen burner on low flame and watch the inner walls of the beaker closely with a magnifying glass.\nStep 3: At 40°C to 60°C (far below the 100°C boiling point), tiny silvery bubbles nucleate on the glass bottom and sides.\nStep 4: Explain bubble origin: cold water dissolves significant air; warming water forces dissolved gas out of solution.\nStep 5: Contrast with boiling at 100°C: massive turbulent steam bubbles rise vigorously from the bottom.\nStep 6: Conclude heating reduces the solubility of gases in liquids.",
         "Confusing low-temperature dissolved air release with 100°C steam boiling.",
         "Opening a warm bottle of soda fizzes violently; opening a cold soda keeps the fizz inside.",
         "Physical activity", "Watch a pot of cold water heat on a kitchen stove: note how early the tiny air bubbles appear on the bottom.",
         "Demonstrate that gas solubility decreases with increasing temperature."),

        ("Testing scratch hardness order of minerals", "A student has four mineral samples: Calcite, Quartz, Talc, and Gypsum. Outline the step-by-step scratch testing sequence to rank them in order of hardness.",
         "Student thinks the biggest, darkest rock is always the hardest.",
         "1. Talc is scratched easily by a fingernail (softest, Mohs 1). 2. Gypsum is scratched by a fingernail with slightly more pressure (Mohs 2). 3. Calcite resists a fingernail but is easily scratched by a copper coin (Mohs 3). 4. Quartz cannot be scratched by a steel nail and scratches glass easily (hardest, Mohs 7).",
         "The Mohs scale ranks minerals by relative scratch hardness: a mineral of higher hardness scratches any mineral below it on the scale.",
         "Step 1: Tool scratch test with Fingernail (hardness ~2.5):\n  - Sample A (Talc) scratches easily, feels greasy -> Rank 1 (Softest)\n  - Sample B (Gypsum) scratches with firm fingernail pressure -> Rank 2\nStep 2: Tool scratch test with Copper Coin (hardness ~3.5):\n  - Sample C (Calcite) resists fingernail, but copper coin leaves a clear white scratch -> Rank 3\nStep 3: Tool scratch test with Steel File / Glass plate (hardness ~5.5-6.5):\n  - Sample D (Quartz) cannot be scratched by steel; Quartz scratches glass easily -> Rank 4 (Hardest)\nStep 4: Compile ascending hardness order: Talc < Gypsum < Calcite < Quartz.",
         "Judging mineral hardness by rock size or color rather than comparative scratch testing.",
         "A metal knife cuts butter, but butter cannot cut metal; harder materials scratch softer ones.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which everyday powder used on babies and carrom boards is made from the softest mineral (Talc)?",
         "Teach the operational mechanics of the Mohs scale of mineral hardness."),

        ("Diagnosing material failure in a broken toy", "A child drops a plastic toy car on a concrete floor in freezing winter (-5°C) and it shatters into pieces, whereas the same toy bounced unharmed in summer (35°C). Explain step-by-step why the material failed.",
         "Student thinks the cold concrete became sharp like glass.",
         "The toy was made of a thermoplastic that undergoes a 'glass transition' at low temperatures: in summer, polymer chains are flexible and absorb impact; in sub-zero winter, polymer chains freeze into rigid, brittle glass-like states, causing the plastic to shatter upon impact.",
         "Many polymers exhibit temperature-dependent mechanical transitions. Above their glass transition temperature (Tg), they are ductile and impact-resistant; below Tg, molecular chains cannot flex, making them brittle.",
         "1. Summer physics (35°C): ambient temperature is above the polymer's glass transition temperature (Tg).\n2. Molecular state: polymer chains slide and flex easily, absorbing the kinetic shock of impact through elastic deformation.\n3. Winter physics (-5°C): temperature drops below Tg.\n4. Molecular freeze: polymer chains lock into rigid crystalline/amorphous configurations with zero flex.\n5. Impact outcome: kinetic energy fractures brittle chemical bonds instantly, shattering the toy into shards.",
         "Assuming material hardness and brittleness are fixed constants independent of temperature.",
         "A warm rubber hose bends smoothly in your hands; freeze the hose in liquid nitrogen and it shatters like a glass light bulb.",
         "None", "What scientific term describes the critical temperature below which a flexible plastic turns brittle like glass?",
         "Introduce polymer glass transition (Tg) and temperature-dependent mechanical properties."),

        ("Selecting materials for an electric toaster", "Deconstruct a kitchen bread toaster into three functional components (heating element, outer casing, crumb tray) and select appropriate materials for each with step-by-step scientific justification.",
         "Student suggests making the heating element out of cotton string.",
         "1. Heating element: Nichrome wire (high electrical resistance, withstands red-hot heat without melting or oxidizing). 2. Outer casing: Heat-resistant plastic or insulated stainless steel (stays cool, electrical insulator). 3. Crumb tray: Galvanized steel or aluminium (fireproof, non-rusting, easy to slide and clean).",
         "Appliance engineering matches specific material electrical, thermal, and mechanical properties to the distinct operating demands of each component.",
         "Step 1: Heating element requirements: must conduct electricity with high resistance to convert electrical energy to red-hot radiant heat (~800°C) without burning or melting.\n  - Choice: Nichrome alloy (80% nickel, 20% chromium) -> optimal high-temperature resistance.\nStep 2: Outer casing requirements: must protect user from 800°C internal heat and electric shock.\n  - Choice: Cool-touch thermoset plastic (Bakelite/PBT) with air gap insulation -> safe to touch.\nStep 3: Crumb tray requirements: collects hot falling crumbs without catching fire, slides smoothly.\n  - Choice: Stamped aluminium or galvanized steel sheet -> fireproof, durable, washable.\nStep 4: Verify complete material compatibility and consumer electrical safety.",
         "Proposing flammable, low-melting, or non-conductive materials for high-temperature electrical heating.",
         "Building a fireplace with heatproof firebricks on the inside and a beautiful cool mantelpiece on the outside.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a cross-section of a toaster showing the Nichrome heating wires, mica sheet support, and insulated outer wall.",
         "Integrate materials selection with appliance design and consumer electrical safety."),

        ("Designing a water-oil separation funnel protocol", "Outline the step-by-step procedure to separate a 100 mL mixture of kerosene oil and water using a glass separating funnel in a science lab.",
         "Student tries to filter the kerosene through filter paper.",
         "1. Pour mixture into closed separating funnel mounted on a retort stand. 2. Allow to stand undisturbed until two crisp layers form. 3. Remove the top stopper. 4. Carefully open the bottom stopcock to drain the lower water layer into a beaker. 5. Close stopcock when oil boundary reaches valve, leaving pure kerosene in the funnel.",
         "Separating funnels exploit immiscibility and density differences to cleanly drain the denser bottom liquid (water) while retaining the lighter floating liquid (keratin/oil) in the funnel.",
         "Step 1: Clamp a glass separating funnel vertically on an iron retort stand; verify the bottom stopcock tap is closed.\nStep 2: Pour the shaken mixture of kerosene and water through the top opening and insert the glass stopper.\nStep 3: Allow the funnel to stand undisturbed for 5 minutes: water (density 1.0 g/cm³) settles at the bottom; kerosene (density ~0.8 g/cm³) floats cleanly on top.\nStep 4: Remove the top glass stopper (essential to allow atmospheric air pressure to displace draining liquid).\nStep 5: Place Beaker 1 under the spout; open stopcock gently: water drains smoothly into Beaker 1.\nStep 6: Watch the descending oil-water boundary closely: close the stopcock the exact instant the boundary reaches the valve hole.\nStep 7: Place Beaker 2 under spout; open stopcock to collect the pure kerosene oil.",
         "Forgetting to remove the top stopper (creating a vacuum that stops liquid draining), or over-draining oil into the water beaker.",
         "Draining sediment from the bottom of an oil tank using a bottom drain tap.",
         "Diagram", "Label the stopper, upper kerosene layer, lower water layer, and stopcock on a diagram of a separating funnel.",
         "Teach precision fluid manipulation using standard laboratory glassware."),

        ("Evaluating suitability of paper for a liquid teacup", "Why can't we use a simple sheet of writing paper to hold hot tea, and how is disposable paper cup material specially modified to solve this?",
         "Student thinks paper cups are made from a secret waterproof tree bark.",
         "Uncoated writing paper is porous and absorbs hot tea through capillary action, turning soggy and leaking within seconds; disposable paper cups are lined on the inside with an ultra-thin bonded coating of polyethylene plastic or wax that blocks liquid penetration.",
         "Paper provides lightweight structural rigidity, but is inherently permeable to liquids. Adding a microscopic plastic barrier film (~15 microns thick) seals the porous cellulose, creating a composite cup that holds hot liquids safely.",
         "1. Raw paper limitation: hydrophilic cellulose fibres absorb water by capillary wicking, breaking inter-fibre hydrogen bonds -> structural collapse and leakage.\n2. Thermal effect: hot liquids accelerate water absorption and soften cellulose faster than cold water.\n3. Composite solution: extrusion-coating paperboard with an ultra-thin layer of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) or plant-based PLA plastic.\n4. Heat-sealing: heat fuses the plastic-coated seams into a leakproof cylinder and disc base.\n5. Outcome: strong, lightweight, leakproof disposable hot beverage cup.",
         "Assuming disposable paper cups are made of 100% plain untreated paper.",
         "Lining a porous wicker basket with a plastic garbage bag so it can hold water like a bucket.",
         "Physical activity", "Peel the paper from a torn disposable coffee cup under water: notice the ultra-thin transparent plastic film inside.",
         "Teach composite barrier packaging and the environmental challenge of recycling plastic-lined paper."),

        ("Sorting plastics for municipal recycling", "A recycling plant receives a mixed bin of plastic scraps: Polyethylene film, PET bottles, and Bakelite switchboards. Step-by-step, explain how workers use heat and density testing to sort them.",
         "Student thinks all plastics are melted together in one giant pot at the same time.",
         "1. Float-sink test in water: Polyethylene film floats (density < 1.0 g/cm³); PET bottles and Bakelite sink (density > 1.0 g/cm³). 2. Heat test on sinking plastics: PET melts at 250°C and can be reshaped (thermoplastic); Bakelite does not melt and chars only at high heat (thermosetting plastic).",
         "Plastics cannot be recycled together because mixing incompatible polymers ruins mechanical strength. Recyclers use physical density stratification followed by thermal classification to isolate pure polymer streams.",
         "Step 1: Shred all mixed plastic into small 1 cm flakes.\nStep 2: Float-sink water bath tank (Density = 1.0 g/cm³):\n  - Polyethylene (PE) and Polypropylene (PP) have density ~0.90-0.95 g/cm³ -> Float to surface -> skimmed off for recycling into plastic pipes.\n  - PET and Bakelite have density > 1.3 g/cm³ -> Sink to bottom.\nStep 3: Collect sunken flakes and dry them.\nStep 4: Thermal melt testing on sunken fraction:\n  - Heat to 260°C: PET flakes melt cleanly into molten polymer -> extruded into new recycled polyester pellets.\n  - Bakelite flakes do not melt (thermosetting polymer) -> filtered out as solid residue and crushed for filler aggregate.",
         "Assuming all plastic waste can be melted together into a single uniform mixture.",
         "Sorting mixed coins by dropping them through different-sized slots, then sorting copper from silver by weight.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a flow chart of a float-sink plastic recycling tank showing floating polyolefins separated from sinking PET.",
         "Connect material physical properties directly to industrial circular recycling engineering.")
    ]

