    # 66..80: Diagram, graph, or virtual-lab reasoning (15)
    diag_data = [
        ("Diagram: Float-Sink Density Tank", "Look at the diagram of a glass water tank containing six submerged and floating objects: Wood block, Wax candle, Apple, Iron coin, Glass marble, and Stone pebble. Classify each object into 'Less dense than water' and 'More dense than water'.",
         "Student guesses based on object size rather than water surface line.",
         "Less dense than water (Floating): Wood block, Wax candle, Apple. More dense than water (Sinking to bottom): Iron coin, Glass marble, Stone pebble.",
         "Any object resting on or protruding above the water surface has an average density less than water (<1.0 g/cm³); any object resting on the bottom floor has a density greater than water (>1.0 g/cm³).",
         "1. Inspect water surface boundary.\n2. Floating objects (partially or fully buoyed at surface): Wood, Wax, Apple -> Density < 1.0 g/cm³.\n3. Note apple buoyancy: apples are 25% air by volume, so they float high on water.\n4. Sunken objects (resting on tank floor): Iron coin, Glass marble, Stone pebble -> Density > 1.0 g/cm³.\n5. Conclude surface position visually communicates relative volumetric density.",
         "Misidentifying an apple as sinking because it is larger than a coin.",
         "Boats and ducks float on the water surface; sunken anchors and lost keys sit on the river bed.",
         "Diagram", "Why does an apple float on water even though it feels heavier in your hand than a glass marble?",
         "Connect visual water tank diagrams with relative density classification."),

        ("Diagram: Optical transmission pathways", "Study the diagram showing light rays from a flashlight passing through three square panels (A, B, C): Panel A lets all straight parallel rays through; Panel B bends and scatters rays in random directions; Panel C reflects or absorbs all rays with zero passage. Label A, B, and C as Transparent, Translucent, or Opaque.",
         "Student thinks Panel B is transparent because light came out of it.",
         "Panel A is Transparent (unscattered parallel transmission); Panel B is Translucent (diffused scattering transmission); Panel C is Opaque (zero light transmission).",
         "Optical categorization depends on light ray trajectories: transparent media maintain parallel ray coherence; translucent media scatter rays randomly; opaque media terminate ray transmission completely.",
         "1. Panel A: incoming parallel rays emerge on the other side completely parallel and unattenuated -> Transparent (e.g. clear glass).\n2. Panel B: incoming rays pass through but are deflected at random chaotic angles -> Translucent (e.g. frosted glass, butter paper).\n3. Panel C: zero rays emerge on the other side; rays are absorbed as heat or reflected -> Opaque (e.g. wood, cardboard).\n4. Conclude ray optics diagrams illustrate the physical basis of optical property definitions.",
         "Confusing diffused scattered light transmission with clear optical transparency.",
         "Car headlights shining through clear air (transparent) versus headlights shining through thick foggy mist (translucent).",
         "Diagram", "Which panel (A, B, or C) would allow you to read a book placed directly behind it?",
         "Teach geometric ray optics representations of material transparency."),

        ("Diagram: Separating funnel layer separation", "In the diagram of a separating funnel containing two liquids, Liquid 1 forms the upper layer and Liquid 2 forms the lower layer. If the two liquids are kerosene oil and water, which liquid is Liquid 1, and why is the stopcock placed at the bottom?",
         "Student labels water as Liquid 1 because water is clean.",
         "Liquid 1 (upper layer) is Kerosene oil (less dense, ~0.8 g/cm³); Liquid 2 (lower layer) is Water (denser, 1.0 g/cm³); the stopcock is at the bottom so the dense water can be drained out first, leaving oil behind.",
         "Gravity naturally stratifies immiscible liquids by density. Placing the draining valve at the lowest point allows controlled withdrawal of the denser bottom phase.",
         "1. Identify density: Water (1.0 g/cm³) > Kerosene (~0.8 g/cm³).\n2. Stratification: the denser liquid (water) sinks to the bottom; the lighter liquid (kerosene) floats on top.\n3. Component identification: Upper Layer 1 = Kerosene; Lower Layer 2 = Water.\n4. Mechanism of operation: opening the bottom stopcock lets water drain out by gravity into a collection flask.\n5. Closing valve: stopcock is closed the instant the interface touches the valve, achieving 100% clean separation.",
         "Swapping the positions of water and kerosene in a separating funnel diagram.",
         "Cream rising to the top of milk: you skim the bottom skimmed milk out through a bottom valve.",
         "Diagram", "What would happen if you tried to drain the upper kerosene layer first without draining the water?",
         "Teach laboratory glassware mechanics and density-driven fluid stratification."),

        ("Graph: Solubility of solids vs water temperature", "A line graph shows solubility (grams dissolved per 100 mL water) against temperature (0°C to 100°C): Sugar rises steeply from 180 g to 480 g; Salt stays nearly flat from 35.7 g to 39.2 g; Chalk stays at 0.001 g across all temperatures. What happens to the solubility of sugar as temperature increases from 20°C to 80°C?",
         "Student says sugar solubility goes down because hot water melts sugar.",
         "Sugar solubility increases dramatically from approx. 200 g to over 360 g per 100 mL as temperature rises from 20°C to 80°C.",
         "Heating liquid water increases kinetic energy and expands intermolecular spaces, allowing water molecules to accommodate far more dissolved sucrose molecules.",
         "1. Locate 20°C on horizontal temperature axis: follow vertically to sugar line -> intersects at ~204 grams.\n2. Locate 80°C on horizontal axis: follow vertically to sugar line -> intersects at ~362 grams.\n3. Calculate increase: solubility nearly doubles (+158 grams per 100 mL).\n4. Contrast with salt: salt line is almost horizontal (temperature has negligible effect on NaCl solubility).\n5. Contrast with chalk: chalk line sits flat at zero (insoluble at all temperatures).",
         "Assuming temperature has the exact same effect on all chemical solutes.",
         "Packing clothes in a suitcase: heating the water is like unzipping the suitcase's expansion zipper to fit more clothes.",
         "Graph", "Which solid's solubility in that graph is almost completely unaffected by heating the water?",
         "Teach data extraction and comparative analysis from solubility curve graphs."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Testing electrical conductivity of everyday objects", "In a virtual lab, a student tests four objects in a circuit with a battery and light bulb: Object 1 (Iron key) -> bulb glows brightly; Object 2 (Plastic ruler) -> bulb stays off; Object 3 (Pencil graphite lead) -> bulb glows brightly; Object 4 (Wooden toothpick) -> bulb stays off. Classify each object as a conductor or insulator.",
         "Student thinks pencil lead is wood, so it should be an insulator.",
         "Conductors (bulb glows): Iron key and Pencil graphite lead. Insulators (bulb off): Plastic ruler and Wooden toothpick.",
         "Metals and graphite have delocalized free electrons that carry electrical current. Plastics and dry wood lack free mobile electrons, completely blocking current flow.",
         "1. Test Object 1 (Iron key): metal with free electrons -> completes circuit -> bulb glows -> Conductor.\n2. Test Object 2 (Plastic ruler): synthetic polymer with tightly bound electrons -> open circuit -> bulb dark -> Insulator.\n3. Test Object 3 (Pencil lead): made of graphite (crystalline carbon with free pi electrons) -> conducts current -> bulb glows -> Conductor.\n4. Test Object 4 (Wooden toothpick): dry plant cellulose with zero mobile charge carriers -> bulb dark -> Insulator.\n5. Note exceptional non-metal conductor: graphite is an extraordinary non-metallic conductor of electricity.",
         "Assuming all non-metals are always electrical insulators (overlooking graphite).",
         "A bridge allows cars to cross a river; a conductor allows electrical electrons to cross the circuit gap.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What material is pencil lead actually made of: lead metal or a mixture of graphite and clay?",
         "Demystify pencil 'lead' and reinforce graphite's unique status as a conducting non-metal."),

        ("Diagram: Freshly cut metal rod showing lustre", "Study the diagram of an old weathered copper pipe cut in half by a hacksaw: the outer cylindrical wall is dark dull green, but the circular cut cross-section gleams with bright reddish-gold shine. Explain why only the cut face shines.",
         "Student thinks the saw blade left gold paint on the cut end.",
         "The outer cylindrical surface has reacted with air and moisture over months, forming an opaque dull green corrosion layer (basic copper carbonate); the newly cut cross-section exposes unreacted pure copper metal, displaying its natural metallic lustre.",
         "Corrosion is a superficial surface phenomenon. Inside the bulk metal, pure metallic crystal bonding is preserved, instantly revealed when mechanically sliced open.",
         "1. Exterior surface condition: exposed to air, moisture, and CO2 for years -> covered in dull green tarnish (patina/verdigris).\n2. Hacksaw cutting action: metal saw teeth physically slice through the pipe wall, exposing internal metallic grain.\n3. Cross-sectional face: pure, unreacted elemental copper atoms exposed to light.\n4. Optical reflection: free conduction electrons reflect incoming light specularly, producing brilliant reddish-gold metallic lustre.\n5. Pedagogical takeaway: always check freshly cut surfaces when identifying weathered metals.",
         "Mistaking superficial environmental tarnish for the intrinsic bulk material property.",
         "Peeling a dirty brown potato skin reveals the clean white potato inside; cutting tarnished copper reveals gleaming metal.",
         "Diagram", "What tool can a student use in a school lab to rub away the dull surface tarnish on an old metal rod without cutting it?",
         "Teach mechanical surface preparation (sandpapering) for lustre verification."),

        ("Graph: Scratch hardness on the Mohs scale", "A bar chart displays Mohs hardness values: Talc = 1, Gypsum = 2, Calcite = 3, Fluorite = 4, Apatite = 5, Feldspar = 6, Quartz = 7, Topaz = 8, Corundum = 9, Diamond = 10. Can a sample of Feldspar (hardness 6) scratch a sample of Fluorite (hardness 4)? Can it scratch Quartz (hardness 7)?",
         "Student thinks softer materials scratch harder materials if you press harder.",
         "Yes, Feldspar (6) will easily scratch Fluorite (4) because 6 > 4; but Feldspar (6) can never scratch Quartz (7) because 6 < 7 (instead, Quartz will scratch the Feldspar).",
         "On the Mohs scale, any material can scratch any other material with an equal or lower hardness number, but cannot scratch a material with a higher number.",
         "1. Identify hardness of Feldspar: 6.\n2. Identify hardness of Fluorite: 4. Compare: 6 > 4 -> Feldspar scratches Fluorite.\n3. Identify hardness of Quartz: 7. Compare: 6 < 7 -> Feldspar cannot scratch Quartz; Quartz scratches Feldspar.\n4. Golden rule of scratch testing: Harder material always scratches the softer material.",
         "Believing scratchability depends on pressing strength rather than inherent crystal lattice hardness.",
         "An adult can carry a child, but a toddler cannot lift an adult; harder minerals mark softer minerals.",
         "Graph", "Which mineral on that graph is the hardest natural substance known on Earth?",
         "Reinforce the directional scratch rule of the Mohs hardness scale."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Testing gas solubility in soda", "In a virtual chemistry lab, a sealed clear bottle of soda water sits quietly with zero bubbles. When the cap is unscrewed, a loud 'hiss' is heard and thousands of vigorous bubbles suddenly form and rise to the top. What gas was dissolved, and why did opening the cap cause bubbles to appear?",
         "Student thinks unscrewing the cap pumped air into the bottle from outside.",
         "Carbon dioxide gas was dissolved under high pressure; unscrewing the cap released the pressure, dramatically reducing CO2 solubility according to Henry's law and forcing excess dissolved gas to come out of solution as bubbles.",
         "Gas solubility in liquids is directly proportional to gas pressure (Henry's law). Factories bottle soda under 3 to 4 atmospheres of CO2 pressure. Opening the cap drops the pressure to 1 atmosphere, so excess gas escapes violently.",
         "1. Factory state: carbon dioxide gas dissolved in flavoured water under high pressure (3-4 atm) inside sealed bottle.\n2. In equilibrium: dissolved CO2 molecules remain invisible in solution without forming bubbles.\n3. Cap removal: pressurized gas in headspace vents out with an audible 'hiss'; headspace pressure drops to 1 atm.\n4. Solubility collapse: cold water cannot hold that much dissolved CO2 at normal atmospheric pressure.\n5. Effervescence: dissolved gas molecules coalesce into microscopic bubbles at nucleation sites and rise rapidly.",
         "Assuming bubbles are created by outside air entering the bottle.",
         "Releasing a compressed spring: under pressure it stays tight; release the clamp and it springs out instantly.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What happens to the rate of bubbling if the opened soda bottle is warmed gently in hot water?",
         "Teach pressure dependence of gas solubility (Henry's law) via everyday soda effervescence."),

        ("Diagram: The Archimedes buoyancy principle", "Look at the diagram showing a 500 cm³ solid metal cylinder submerged in a water overflow can: 500 mL of water overflows into a measuring beaker. What is the volume of water displaced, and how does displaced volume relate to object volume?",
         "Student thinks the water overflowed because the metal was hot.",
         "The volume of water displaced is exactly 500 mL (500 cm³); any completely submerged object displaces a volume of liquid exactly equal to its own submerged solid volume.",
         "Matter cannot occupy the exact same space at the same time. When a solid enters liquid, it pushes aside an identical volume of liquid molecules into the overflow spout.",
         "1. Initial state: overflow displacement can is filled with water exactly to the lip of the spout.\n2. Immersion: 500 cm³ solid metal cylinder is lowered completely below the water line.\n3. Displacement: solid cylinder takes up 500 cm³ of space previously occupied by liquid water.\n4. Overflow collection: displaced water spills through spout into collection beaker.\n5. Measured volume: collection beaker reads exactly 500 mL (1 mL = 1 cm³).\n6. Fundamental principle: Submerged Volume of Object = Volume of Displaced Liquid.",
         "Believing heavy objects displace more liquid volume than light objects of identical size.",
         "Putting on a shoe: your foot pushes an exact foot-shaped volume of air out of the shoe.",
         "Diagram", "If you submerge an irregular 50 cm³ stone in this full overflow can, how many mL of water will spill into the beaker?",
         "Establish the fundamental volume-displacement foundation of hydrostatics."),

        ("Graph: Density comparison across solid materials", "A horizontal bar chart displays densities (g/cm³): Gold = 19.3, Lead = 11.3, Iron = 7.8, Aluminium = 2.7, Glass = 2.5, Water = 1.0, Ice = 0.92, Wood (Pine) = 0.50, Cork = 0.24. Draw a vertical line at Water (1.0). Which materials float, and which material has the highest density?",
         "Student thinks Aluminium floats because aluminium cans feel very light in hand.",
         "Floating materials (density < 1.0): Cork (0.24), Pine Wood (0.50), Ice (0.92). Sinking materials (density > 1.0): Aluminium, Glass, Iron, Lead, Gold. Gold has the highest density (19.3 g/cm³).",
         "The vertical line at 1.0 g/cm³ serves as nature's flotation threshold: everything to the left floats on water; everything to the right sinks. Even though aluminium is a 'lightweight' metal (2.7 g/cm³), it is nearly three times denser than water and sinks.",
         "1. Locate benchmark: vertical line at Water = 1.0 g/cm³.\n2. Left of line (Flotation zone): Cork (0.24) < Wood (0.50) < Ice (0.92) < 1.0 -> all float.\n3. Right of line (Sinking zone): Glass (2.5) < Aluminium (2.7) < Iron (7.8) < Lead (11.3) < Gold (19.3) -> all sink.\n4. Maximum density: Gold bar reaches 19.3 g/cm³ (almost 20 times denser than water).\n5. Clarify aluminium: light for a metal, but denser than water, so solid aluminium sinks.",
         "Assuming lightweight metals like aluminium float on water.",
         "A boat floats on the left side of the pier; an anchor sinks on the right side.",
         "Graph", "If you drop a solid aluminium spoon in water, will it float or sink? Explain using the graph.",
         "Strengthen quantitative density threshold analysis using comparative charts."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Simulating miscibility of three liquids", "In a virtual chemistry lab, three unlabelled liquids (P, Q, R) are tested: P + Water -> forms two layers (P floats); Q + Water -> forms a single clear phase; R + Water -> forms two layers (R sinks to bottom). Identify P, Q, and R from: Honey, Mustard oil, and Alcohol.",
         "Student thinks Honey floats on top because it is sweet.",
         "P is Mustard oil (immiscible, less dense than water); Q is Alcohol (miscible with water); R is Honey (immiscible/slow-dissolving, denser than water, sinks to bottom).",
         "Liquids separate by density and mix by polarity. Oil is hydrophobic and lighter than water (floats). Alcohol is polar and forms hydrogen bonds with water (miscible). Heavy dense honey sinks directly to the floor.",
         "1. Analyze Liquid P: immiscible + floats on water -> low density (<1.0 g/cm³) non-polar liquid -> Mustard oil.\n2. Analyze Liquid Q: miscible + forms single clear homogeneous solution -> polar liquid -> Alcohol (or vinegar).\n3. Analyze Liquid R: settles straight to the bottom under water -> high density (>1.4 g/cm³) viscous liquid -> Honey.\n4. Conclude three distinct liquid-water interactions observed.",
         "Confusing liquid viscosity with density (assuming thick honey must float).",
         "Dropping a penny and a leaf in water: the leaf floats on top, the penny sinks to the bottom.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What happens to the sunken honey in Beaker R if you stir it vigorously for 2 minutes?",
         "Demonstrate that dense soluble liquids eventually dissolve when mechanically agitated."),

        ("Diagram: Cross-section of a high-voltage electrical cable", "Examine the technical cross-section diagram of an overhead power transmission cable: show the central steel reinforcing core, the concentric aluminium conductor strands, and the thick XLPE insulating sheath. Match each component to its required material property.",
         "Student thinks the entire cable is made of rubber.",
         "Steel core = High tensile strength (prevents long cables from snapping under wind and weight); Aluminium strands = High electrical conductivity + light weight; XLPE outer sheath = High electrical insulation + weather resistance.",
         "Power cable design is a masterclass in composite material selection: aluminium conducts current without heavy copper weight, steel provides tensile backbone against sagging, and polymer shields against electrocution.",
         "1. Center: high-strength galvanized steel wire -> Tensile strength property (withstands span tension between towers).\n2. Middle layer: multi-strand aluminium wires -> Electrical conductivity + low density property (transports current with minimal tower load).\n3. Outer jacket: cross-linked polyethylene (XLPE) or rubber -> Electrical insulation property (prevents arcing and protects workers).\n4. Conclude no single material could satisfy all three engineering requirements simultaneously.",
         "Assuming electrical cables only require conductivity without mechanical structural support.",
         "A human arm has bone for strength (steel), muscles for action (aluminium), and skin for protection (insulator).",
         "Diagram", "Why is aluminium used for long-distance overhead power cables instead of copper, even though copper is a slightly better conductor?",
         "Connect material density, electrical conductivity, and structural engineering cost."),

        ("Graph: Porosity vs water absorption percentage in building bricks", "A bar graph shows percentage of water absorbed after 24 hours immersion: Glazed ceramic tile = 0.5%, Concrete paver = 5%, Standard red clay brick = 18%, Autoclaved aerated concrete block = 45%. Which brick material absorbs the most water, and why is glazed tile used for bathroom walls?",
         "Student thinks glazed tiles absorb all the bathroom water to keep the room dry.",
         "Autoclaved aerated concrete absorbs the most water (45%) due to massive internal air pores; glazed ceramic tile absorbs virtually zero water (0.5%) because its glass-vitrified glaze is completely non-porous and waterproof, preventing bathroom walls from rotting.",
         "Bathroom walls require impermeable materials. Porous red bricks absorb moisture, breeding black mold and crumbling plaster. Glazed tiles provide a vitrified waterproof barrier that sheds water directly down the drain.",
         "1. Read bar heights: Aerated block (45%) > Red brick (18%) > Concrete paver (5%) > Glazed tile (0.5%).\n2. High porosity cause: aerated blocks contain millions of microscopic gas bubbles that suck up water by capillary action.\n3. Vitrification: firing ceramic tiles with liquid silica glaze melts into an impermeable glass shield.\n4. Architectural application: non-porous glazed tiles prevent dampness, water seepage, and fungal growth in bathrooms.",
         "Misunderstanding the functional purpose of waterproof glazed tiles in domestic sanitation.",
         "Putting a raincoat on a house wall so water slides off instead of soaking through the bricks.",
         "Graph", "What happens to a building wall if porous bricks without waterproof plaster are exposed to heavy monsoon rains?",
         "Teach porosity, capillary absorption, and architectural material selection via bar charts."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Simulating the scratch hardness test on glass", "In a virtual lab, a student tests five tools against a flat sheet of window glass: Tool 1 (Fingernail) -> zero scratch; Tool 2 (Copper coin) -> zero scratch; Tool 3 (Iron nail) -> zero scratch; Tool 4 (Hardened steel file) -> faint scratch; Tool 5 (Diamond-tipped glass cutter) -> deep clean groove that snaps glass. Where does window glass rank relative to these tools?",
         "Student thinks an iron nail can easily carve glass like butter.",
         "Window glass is harder than a fingernail, copper coin, and iron nail; it has approximately the same hardness as a steel file (~5.5-6.0 Mohs), and is far softer than diamond (10 Mohs), which is why glass-cutters use diamond tips.",
         "Most people assume glass is soft because it shatters easily. In reality, glass has high scratch hardness (Mohs 5.5), easily resisting fingernails, coins, and iron nails. Only harder tools (hardened steel, tungsten carbide, diamond) can scratch it.",
         "1. Test 1 (Fingernail, Mohs 2.5): slides off glass with zero mark -> Glass is harder than fingernail.\n2. Test 2 (Copper coin, Mohs 3.5): slides off with zero mark -> Glass is harder than copper.\n3. Test 3 (Iron nail, Mohs 4.5): cannot scratch glass surface -> Glass is harder than common iron.\n4. Test 4 (Hardened steel, Mohs 6.5): leaves a light scratch -> Glass hardness is ~5.5.\n5. Test 5 (Diamond tip, Mohs 10): effortlessly carves a deep line -> Diamond is vastly harder than glass.\n6. Industrial application: diamond-tipped wheels are the universal standard for cutting commercial glass panes.",
         "Confusing brittle impact fragility with low surface scratch hardness.",
         "A porcelain teacup breaks if dropped on tile, but a steel fork cannot scratch its shiny glaze.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Why does a glazier use a diamond-tipped pencil tool to score and cut large glass sheets?",
         "Correct the persistent confusion between scratch hardness and impact brittleness."),

        ("Diagram: Classification tree of matter", "Study the tree diagram of matter: Matter divides into Pure Substances (Elements and Compounds) and Mixtures (Homogeneous and Heterogeneous). Where do Saltwater, Pure Gold, Tap water, and Granite rock belong?",
         "Student puts tap water under pure substances because it looks clean.",
         "Pure Gold = Element (Pure substance); Saltwater = Homogeneous mixture (Solution); Tap water = Homogeneous mixture (contains dissolved minerals); Granite rock = Heterogeneous mixture (visible flecks of quartz, mica, feldspar).",
         "Classification hierarchies structure all matter in the universe based on chemical uniformity and phase boundaries. Even clear tap water is chemically a mixture of H2O and dissolved mineral salts.",
         "1. Pure Gold: composed of 100% identical gold atoms (Au) -> Element (Pure Substance).\n2. Pure Compound: pure distilled water (H2O) -> Compound.\n3. Saltwater & Tap water: uniform composition throughout, but consists of water plus dissolved salts that can be physically separated -> Homogeneous Mixture.\n4. Granite rock: non-uniform composition with distinct visible mineral crystals -> Heterogeneous Mixture.\n5. Conclude hierarchical classification organizes all physical matter systematically.",
         "Confusing visually clean drinking water with chemically pure distilled H2O.",
         "A bag containing only red marbles (pure element) versus a trail mix with nuts, raisins, and chocolate (heterogeneous mixture).",
         "Diagram", "Why is sea water classified as a mixture rather than a pure chemical compound?",
         "Establish the foundational classification taxonomy of matter in elementary science.")
    ]

